pySPLIT
=======

This project is an unofficial Python reimplementation ("port") of the SPLIT
R package:

    SPLIT: Spatial Purification of Layered Intracellular Transcripts
    Copyright (c) Mariia Bilous and contributors
    Biomedical Data Science Center, CHUV / University of Lausanne
    https://github.com/bdsc-tds/SPLIT
    Licensed under GPL (>= 3)

The SPLIT method and algorithm are the work of its original authors, published
as:

    Bilous M, Buszta D, Bac J, Kang S, Dong Y, Tissot S, Andre S,
    Alexandre-Gaveta M, Voize C, Peters S, Homicsko K, Gottardo R.
    "Resolving sensitivity, specificity and signal contamination in Xenium
    spatial transcriptomics." Nature Methods (2026).
    doi:10.1038/s41592-026-03089-8

pySPLIT contributes only a translation of that R code into Python, together
with an AnnData-based interface, a sparse-aware reimplementation of the core
purification loop, and tests. It is not affiliated with or endorsed by the
original authors. The R package remains the reference implementation.

Users of pySPLIT should always cite the paper above for the method. This
implementation may additionally be cited as software; see CITATION.cff and the
"Citation" section of README.md.

As a derivative of GPL-licensed work, pySPLIT is distributed under the same
terms: GPL-3.0-or-later. See LICENSE.
